Along with the increased life expectancy characteristic of recent human history, with WHO projecting that one in six people will be aged 60 or more by 2030 [cite], is the prevalence of chronic diseases: a 2025 review reveals that about half of older adults experiences multimorbidity, i.e. suffers from multiple chronic conditions at the same time [cite], which usually require ongoing medication therapies. However, this type of treatment can only succeed if the patients comply with it.
Medication adherence can be formally defined as “the process by which patients take their medications as prescribed, composed of initiation, implementation and discontinuation” [cite]. [cite] reports that between 44% and 76% of patients is non-adherent, with consequences that can be especially severe for elderly patients, resulting in negative health outcomes such as an increase in hospitalization and mortality, as well as in increased healthcare costs. Notably, the problem is accentuated in patients living at home.
The main reasons for medication non-adherence are the complexity of medical non-adherence; forgetting, misunderstanding dosing instructions; intentional opposition to treatment. Many efforts are going into designing smart medication and pill dispensers as a strategy to improve medication adherence by tackling these very reasons.

According to [cite - 15], the majority of existing or proposed systems consist of:
•	A health record system for hospitals
•	A patient information system (used by caregivers to monitor the patient)
•	A smart drug container
•	A mobile tracking system
Most of the solutions use Arduino or Raspberry Pi as enabling technology, with GSM, Wi-Fi or Bluetooth as preferred options for communication. Any smart pill dispenser must also use sensors to detect the presence of pills and whether they have been taken or not, and there must be a physical interface to interact with the devices, usually consisting of buttons, LEDs, speakers or buzzers and even touchscreens.
Most products are accompanied by a survey study, all of which report an increase in medication adherence during device usage [cite - 68], indicating that this is the right direction to proceed; however, there are open issues and challenges that need to be addressed, mainly concerning hardware-software compatibility, the lack of authentication systems to prevent access to unauthorized users (like children) and general accessibility [cite - review]. In particular, very few solutions adopt voice-controlled or easily customizable interfaces. Furthermore, modularity and context-adaptability seem to remain an open issue, with most of the available literature still at proposal or prototype stage.

